Full Debian Sage build for the ECL transition
============================================
Checked 2026-10-12 in a Debian unstable arm64 chroot, using CPUs 0-3.
This uses Debian's source package and dpkg-buildpackage, with Debian
dependencies from apt. Homebrew is not used inside the chroot.

Key versions:
  Sage source: 10.9-4 (UNRELEASED), based on Debian 10.9-3.
  ECL/libecl-dev: 26.5.5+dfsg-1, with the side-module patch.
  maxima-sage: 5.50.0-3, rebuilt against ECL 26.5.
  Python: 3.14; PARI: 2.19.0-1; cypari2: 2.2.6-1.

The native build initially failed because PARI's coeff macro expanded
NTL function calls in ntlwrap_impl.h. Backport u0-pari-ntl-coeff.patch
uses the exact upstream correction from Sage #42888:
https://github.com/sagemath/sage/pull/42888
https://github.com/sagemath/sage/commit/cd2cb16d82d549e21bdbe1361aa3c73f9646f43d
The affected wrapper is unchanged by this ECL transition.

After that backport, the complete native build produced and installed
the CPython 3.14 arm64 Sage wheel into Debian's package staging tree.
The HTML documentation build also completed, including a repeat under
an unprivileged dedicated build user.

The actual full Meson configuration discovers ECL and maxima-sage and
generates MAXIMA_FAS=''. The freshly built Sage passes rational-to-PARI
conversion, sqrt(2)'s minimal polynomial, NTL finite-field and large
integer polynomial checks, and the focused ECL/Maxima autopkgtest.
The latter includes interrupts and divergent-integral handling.
These checks pass as an unprivileged user. readelf confirms that the
new ECL extension links to libecl.so.26.5 and the PARI extensions use
libpari-gmp-tls.so.10, resolving the old installed Sage's PARI ABI mismatch.

The full source doctest run covered 3885 files using four workers.
All 204 ECL and 227 embedded Maxima doctests pass in this full rebuild.
The subsequent pytest run passes all 94 tests, with two skipped.
Debian's failed-example counter reports 917 ordinary failures and two
critical failures. Its normal allowance is 2000 ordinary failures, but
critical failures block the package build.

python3-lrcalc is recommended by the binary package but omitted from
Build-Depends. It was absent in the initial run. Installing the archive's
Python 3.14-compatible python3-lrcalc 2.1-3 clears the affected combinatorics
tests in Debian's automatic serial retry. That retry covers 105 source
files, reports 434 failed examples, and again passes all 94 pytest tests.
The two critical failures recur, and dpkg-buildpackage exits with status 2
at override_dh_auto_test-arch. Documentation doctests are not reached.

The critical failures are:

  1. A PARI-backed hyperelliptic calculation raises SignalError:
     Segmentation fault. It also fails in a fresh Sage process before
     sage.libs.ecl is imported, then reproduces with cypari2 alone and
     directly in GP 2.19.0. The same finite-field model succeeds with
     curve variable x and crashes with t. See README.pari-hyperell-bug
     for the minimal upstream report draft. No workaround is applied.

  2. symbolic/expression.pyx times out at the exact integration example
     integrate(log(1+4/5*sin(x)),x,-3.1415,3.1415), line 13163. This is the
     previously reproduced Maxima 5.50 issue, also present with ECL 24.5:
     https://bugs.debian.org/1150619

The diagnostic run uses SAGE_TIMEOUT_LONG=300 instead of Debian's
3600-second default. This limits conclusions about timeouts under the
default policy; the independently reproduced GP crash is not caused by
the shorter time limit. Other failures include PARI 2.19 API/output changes,
Maxima expression changes, and upstream CLI expectations that do not match
Debian's launcher. A clean complete Sage testsuite is not established.

Binary packaging with DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS='parallel=4 nocheck' succeeds and
produces all five packages: python3-sage, python3-sage-dbgsym, sagemath,
sagemath-doc, and sagemath-jupyter. This does not make the failed normal
test gate pass. The library and launcher packages were installed through
apt in the chroot, replacing the archive's 10.9-3 packages. Outside the
build tree and without PYTHONPATH overrides, the installed Sage passes
the same PARI, NTL, and ECL/Maxima smoke checks, including interrupts and
divergent integrals. Its ECL extension is loaded from /usr/lib/python3/
dist-packages. The installed /usr/bin/sage launcher computes 6*7 as 42.
